 ## Overview
-The Deformation MLP Configuration defines the architecture, initialization, and conditioning mechanisms for a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) used to predict spatial or temporal deformations in neural volume representations. It solves the problem of mapping canonical coordinates to deformed coordinates (or vice versa) by providing a structured way to inject instance-specific or time-specific information into the network's feature space. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [MLP with Input Skips](1643-mlp-with-input-skips.md) by its explicit use of feature-wise modulation (often via Conditional Batch Normalization or FiLM) to handle conditional inputs, and from [Residual Block with Shortcut (ReLU MLP Extension)](10016-residual-block-with-shortcut-relu-mlp-extension.md) by its focus on deformation dynamics rather than static residual learning.
+The Deformation MLP Configuration defines the architecture, initialization, and conditioning mechanisms for a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) used to predict spatial or temporal deformations in neural volume representations [S1]. It solves the problem of mapping canonical coordinates to deformed coordinates by providing a structured way to inject instance-specific or time-specific information into the network's feature space via feature-wise linear modulation [S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [MLP with Input Skips](1643-mlp-with-input-skips.md) by its explicit use of feature-wise modulation to handle conditional inputs, and from [Residual Block with Shortcut (ReLU MLP Extension)](10016-residual-block-with-shortcut-relu-mlp-extension.md) by its focus on deformation dynamics rather than static residual learning [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method configures an MLP to output a displacement vector $\delta\mathbf{x}$ given input coordinates and optional conditioning signals. The configuration involves specific architectural choices, initialization protocols, and conditioning strategies.
+The method configures an MLP to output a displacement vector $\delta\mathbf{x}$ given input coordinates and optional conditioning signals [S1]. The configuration involves specific architectural choices, initialization protocols, and conditioning strategies [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
